<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!-- Generated by Pandoc using pretext.lua -->
<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec-solutions6">
    <title>Dilution calculations</title>

    <p>
        It is common for solutions to become diluted in science and medicine.
        <term><xref ref="def-dilution" text="custom">Dilution</xref></term> is what happens when more solvent is added to an existing
        solution.
        The solute amount remains the same, but the amount of solvent increases,
        and thus the concentration of the solute in the new solution is lower
        than it was previously.
        When dilutions occur, it is helpful to be able to determine what the new
        concentration of the final solution will be.
        Likewise, if you are trying to use dilution to prepare a solution with a
        desired concentration, then you need to know how much of the original
        solution and solvent you should mix.
        To aid in these calculations we have the following formula:
    </p>

    <p>
       <me> C_1\cdot V_1 = C_2\cdot V_2</me>
    </p>

    <p>
        The C's are concentration and the V's are volume.
        <m>C_1</m> and <m>V_1</m> represent the concentration of the starting solution and the
        volume of it that was diluted.
        <m>C_2</m> and <m>V_2</m> represent the concentration of the final diluted solution and
        the volume of the final diluted solution.
        The units on the volumes can be any volume units, as long as the units
        of <m>V_1</m> and <m>V_2</m> are the same.
        Likewise, the units on <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> can be any concentration unit, as long
        as the units are the same.
    </p>
    <exercises>
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>17\ \text{mL}</m> of <m>2.0\ \text{M}</m> sodium chloride is diluted to a final volume of
                    <m>350\ \text{mL}</m>.
                    What is the concentration of the diluted solution?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>0.097\ \text{M}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>28\ \text{mL}</m> of <m>2.0\%\ \text{m/v}</m> sodium hydroxide is diluted to a final
                    volume of <m>2.0\ \text{L}</m>.
                    What is the concentration of the diluted solution?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>0.028\%\ \text{m/v}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>75\ \text{mL}</m> of <m>150.0\ \text{mg}/\text{mL}</m> aspirin is mixed with <m>155\ \text{mL}</m> of water.
                    What is the new concentration?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>49\ \text{mg}/\text{mL}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    You are trying to prepare <m>3.0\ \text{L}</m> of <m>0.050\ \text{M}</m> <m>NaOH</m> by diluting a
                    <m>2.0\ \text{M}</m> solution of <m>NaOH</m>.
                    How many liters of <m>2.0\ \text{M}</m> <m>NaOH</m> do you need to dilute?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>0.075\ \text{L}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    You are trying to prepare <m>3.0\ \text{L}</m> of <m>15\ \text{mM}</m> <m>NaOH</m> by diluting a
                    <m>1.0\ \text{M}</m> solution of <m>NaOH</m>.
                    How many liters of <m>1.0\ \text{M}</m> <m>NaOH</m> do you need to dilute?
                    How many liters of water will you need to add to that?
                    Don’t worry about significant figures for this problem.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>0.045\ \text{L}</m> of <m>1\ \text{M}</m> <m>NaOH</m> needs to be mixed with <m>2.955\ \text{L}</m> of water.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</section>